# BadaBhai alias RETIREMENTS — reviewed decisions that an alias must stop routing workers.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The alias corpus can only add. `db:seed:domains` inserts
# ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING and never deletes, and production rows are never deleted anyway
# (CLAUDE.md §10). So a wrong alias — a published title split into junk ("Machine" off
# "Milker, Machine"), or a phrase a ruling moved to another trade — kept routing workers
# no matter what any file said. A line here takes it out of retrieval instead.
#
# WHAT A LINE DOES. Nothing is deleted. The row stays in the database with its id and its
# embedding; it only stops being searchable:
#   - offline, `resolveJobDomainCorpus` drops it, so the eval, the reachability pins and the
#     chip-label tests measure the routing workers will actually get;
#   - live, `db:normalize:aliases --apply` clears `is_searchable` on it — the flag every
#     retrieval layer filters on — and re-derives that from this file on every run, so no
#     later seed or renormalize can quietly bring it back.
# To undo a retirement, delete its line and re-run the normalizer.
#
# FORMAT. One retirement per line. Every field is required.
#   {"kind":"retire","job_domain_id":"jd_nco_6121_0800","text":"Machine","lang":"en",
#    "ruling":"RVM worksheet Part 5, A4","reason":"comma-split junk of 'Milker, Machine'",
#    "decided_by":"Divyanshu","decided_on":"2026-09-24"}
# (one line in the file — wrapped here only for reading)
#
# THE RULES, all enforced by `validateAliasRetirements`:
#   - `text` + `lang` must match an alias on that domain EXACTLY, published or authored.
#     A line that names nothing would retire nothing while reading as a decision taken.
#   - The effect covers every spelling on that domain that normalizes to the same key,
#     because retrieval matches on the normalized text.
#   - A retirement may not remove a selectable occupation's LAST alias. If workers should
#     reach it by another phrase, add that phrase to rvm-aliases.jsonl in the same change.
#   - `ruling` must name the signed decision it implements. Retiring a phrase changes who
#     gets which form; that is an owner decision, never an engineering tidy-up.
#
# SHIPPING ONE (runbook P3-0): merge, then `db:seed:domains` (no-op for a retirement-only
# change) and `db:normalize:aliases --apply` against production, then `db:verify:domains`,
# which FAILS while any retired alias is still searchable.
